is_scheme_valid(s) is a pure function of the byte string s, with no trimming, no case folding, no percent-decoding, and no length limit:

fn is_scheme_valid(s: &[u8]) -> bool {
    if s.is_empty() { return false; }                 // empty => false
    // first byte must be an ASCII letter
    if !(s[0].is_ascii_alphabetic()) { return false; }  // i.e. 'A'..='Z' | 'a'..='z'
    // every remaining byte must be ASCII alphanumeric or '+' or '-' or '.'
    for &c in &s[1..] {
        let ok = c.is_ascii_alphanumeric()            // 'A'..='Z' | 'a'..='z' | '0'..='9'
              || c == b'+' || c == b'-' || c == b'.';
        if !ok { return false; }
    }
    true
}

Equivalent regex over bytes (anchored, byte-wise, ASCII only): ^[A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9+.-]*$

Exhaustively verified character classes:
- Position 0 (first char): VALID only for the 52 bytes A-Z (0x41-0x5A) and a-z (0x61-0x7A). INVALID for every other byte: all controls 0x01-0x1F, space 0x20, all punctuation/symbols 0x21-0x40 and 0x5B-0x60 and 0x7B-0x7E (including '0'-'9', '+', '-', '.', '_', '~', '%', ':'), DEL-range not tested (unreachable via argv along with NUL), and all high bytes 0x80-0xFF (all 128 tested, all invalid — so no locale-dependent isalpha; behaves as pure ASCII / "C" locale).
- Positions >= 1 (tested at index 1, 2, 4, and at index k for k=0..9 in a 10-char scheme, plus index 299 of a 300-char scheme): VALID only for A-Z, a-z, 0-9, '+' (0x2B), '-' (0x2D), '.' (0x2E). INVALID for everything else, including '_' (0x5F), '~' (0x7E), '%' (0x25), ':' (0x3A), '/' (0x2F), '*', '!', '=', ',', ';', '@', '$', '&', '\'', '(', ')', '[', ']', '^', '{', '|', '}', '<', '>', '"', '`', '\\', all controls 0x01-0x1F, space, and all 128 high bytes 0x80-0xFF.
- The rule for positions >= 1 is uniform: there is no distinction between index 1 and later indices, and no "last character" special case (trailing '+', '-', '.' are all accepted: "a-", "a.", "a+", "a...", "a---", "a+++" are all valid).
- No length limit and no minimum beyond 1: schemes of length 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 8, 16, 31, 32, 33, 63, 64, 65, 100, 127, 128, 129, 200, 255, 256, 257, 511, 512, 1000, 2000, 5000, 20000 (all 'a') are ALL valid. A 300-char mixed scheme "a" + repeating "+-.9" is valid; appending a single '_' to it makes it invalid.
- Case-insensitive in the sense that upper and lower letters are equally accepted; there is no lowercase requirement and no lowercasing happens before the check (both "HTTP" and "http" are valid; validity of a mixed-case scheme like "hTtP" is unchanged).
- No whitespace tolerance / no trim() anywhere on this path: " http", "http ", "\thttp", "http\t", "\nhttp", "http\n", "\rhttp", "http\r", "\vhttp", "\fhttp", "  http  " are ALL invalid schemes. (Corollary about parse(): the raw input URL is NOT trimmed either — ' http://host' yields scheme=" http" and LINE 2 = 0.)
- No known-scheme whitelist: single-letter schemes "a", "A", "z", "Z", "x" are all valid.

Note for anyone re-running probes of the form "S://host": if S contains '#' or '?' the fragment/query is stripped from the whole URL BEFORE the '://' split, so the parse is perturbed (e.g. 'ab#://host' => 1|0|0|0|1|[ab] — LINE 2 = 0 there because scheme becomes empty, not because '#' was rejected inside a scheme). Similarly S containing ':' or '/' shifts where the first "://" is found and thus truncates the scheme actually handed to is_scheme_valid. All conclusions above were taken only from probes whose LINE 3 = 1 and LINE 5 = 0 (confirming host still parsed as "host").

Randomized validation: 400 random schemes over a 39-character alphabet (letters, digits, '+', '-', '.', '_', '~', '%', and many other punctuation marks), lengths 1-6, excluding any containing ':', '/', '#', '?': predicate ^[A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9+.-]*$ matched the binary's LINE 2 in 400/400 cases, 0 mismatches.
